---
import AdeGuide from '../../../components/ade/AdeGuide.astro';
import { law, fees } from '../../../data/ade-eligibility';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Can an adult get a learner permit in Texas?', a: 'Texas learner licenses are issued under §521.222 to applicants 15 or older but under 18. An adult applies for the driver license itself rather than a learner license first, so the teen two-step does not apply to you.' },
  { q: 'How do I practice driving legally before my test then?', a: 'Through a driving school, which provides an instructor and a dual-control car, or supervised practice where your circumstances permit it. What you cannot do is drive unsupervised without a license to build up practice — that is the thing the learner license exists to authorise for teenagers.' },
  { q: 'Do I need to hold anything for six months like a teenager does?', a: 'The six-month holding period in Texas law attaches to a learner license, and learner licenses are a minor’s route. Adults are not serving a waiting period of that kind.' },
  { q: 'What about the "restricted driver license" DPS mentions for third-party testing?', a: 'DPS lists a valid restricted driver license among the requirements for third-party skills testing at 18 and over. If you hold no Texas credential at all, ask the school or DPS what applies to your situation before booking — this is the one corner of the adult path where the wording is genuinely unclear, and we would rather say so than guess.' },
  { q: 'Do I still need the six-hour course?', a: `If you are ${law.requiredBand} and this is a first license, yes. Your practice arrangements do not change that.` },
];
---
<AdeGuide
  title="Is There an Adult Learner Permit in Texas? | Road Ready Safety"
  description="Texas learner licenses are for applicants aged 15 to 17. Adults apply for the license directly — what that means for practising, and the one piece of DPS wording worth asking about."
  url="/adult-drivers-ed/texas/adult-learner-permit-texas"
  eyebrow="Eligibility"
  h1="Is there an adult learner permit in Texas?"
  lede="Short answer: not in the way teenagers have one. The learner license is a minor's route, and that changes how an adult should think about getting practice before the driving test."
  faqs={faqs}
  sources={[
    { label: 'Tex. Transp. Code §521.222, Learner License', href: 'https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/TN/htm/TN.521.htm' },
    { label: 'Tex. Transp. Code §521.204, Restrictions on Minor', href: 'https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/TN/htm/TN.521.htm' },
    { label: 'Texas DPS, Third Party Skills Testing Program', href: 'https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/third-party-skills-testing-program' },
  ]}
>
  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">What the statute says</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      §521.222 authorizes a learner license for a person who &ldquo;is 15 years of age or older but
      under 18 years of age&rdquo;, who has passed the classroom phase of an approved driver
      education course and every examination other than the driving test. It is the first half of the
      graduated licensing system Texas built for teenagers.
    </p>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      As an adult you are outside that system. You apply for the driver license, take the tests, and
      are issued the license. There is no intermediate permit stage designed for you and no six-month
      holding period to serve.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">Which leaves a practical problem</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      The learner license exists so a teenager can legally accumulate driving hours before the test.
      An adult who has never driven needs the same hours and has no equivalent instrument. The
      realistic routes are a commercial driving school, which supplies an instructor and a
      dual-control car, or supervised practice where your circumstances allow it.
    </p>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      What is not a route is driving unlicensed to get the practice. It is the one shortcut that can
      end with a conviction rather than a license.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">One piece of wording worth asking about</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      DPS&rsquo;s third-party skills testing page lists &ldquo;a valid restricted driver license&rdquo;
      among the requirements for applicants 18 and over. What that means for someone holding no Texas
      credential at all is not spelled out on the page, and we are not going to invent an answer.
      If you are planning to use a third-party road test, ask the school or DPS how it applies to you
      before you pay anything.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">Read next</h2>
    <ul class="flex flex-col gap-3 pl-5 m-0 list-disc marker:text-text-subtle">
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]"><a class="underline hover:no-underline" href="/adult-drivers-ed/texas/never-had-a-license">Getting a first license as an adult who has never driven</a></li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]"><a class="underline hover:no-underline" href="/adult-drivers-ed/texas/how-to-get-a-texas-license-as-an-adult">The full process</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</AdeGuide>
